 HSITOOL32: Sega CARTDEV/Game    Written by The Igneous Group, Geoff Caras.

 >>> Press the ESC key to exit help at any time. <<<


        Command        Description
  ___________________________________________________________________________
        //             Comment, the rest of the line is ignored.
        ?              Displays this help message
        @              Redirects input to a file
        audioread      Reads Digital Audio Data into a file.
        abort          Aborts the last SCSI command (experimental)
        batch          Allows for control of flow when running a script.
        buffconfig     Displays the CARTDEV buffer configuration.
        cmpdpr         Compares DPR memory to internal buffers.
        compare        Compares Game memory.
        configbuf      Allows setting CARTDEV buffer config table entries.
        controlgm      Issues a control game command to the CARTDEV.
        delay          Delays command processing.
        download       Downloads the monitor to the GAME.
        dump           Displays Game memory.
        enter          Edits memory
        exit           Exits HSITOOL32.
*
        Command        Description
  ___________________________________________________________________________

        fill           Fills Game memory with a value.
        flash          Flash programming of the CARTDEV Flash EEPROM.
        fromaddr       Gets data from CARTDEV memory.
        fromdpr        Gets data from CARTDEV DPR memory and puts into buffer.
        gameint        Enables or disables the interrupts to the GAME.
        go             Sends the GO command to a target processor.
        help           Displays this message text.
        inquiry        Displays SCSI inquiry data.
        lastsense      Prints the SCSI sense data for the last SCSI command.
        loopback       Runs a loopback test to the GAME system.
        memtest        Runs the monitor memory test.
        move           Moves Game memory.
        quit           Exits HSITOOL32.
        readb          Reads GAME memory as bytes.
        readl          Reads GAME memory as long words.
        readw          Reads GAME memory as words.
        register       Displays the GAMES systems' processor registers.
        reqsense       Sends a SCSI Request Sense command to CARTDEV.
*
        Command        Description
  ___________________________________________________________________________
        reset          Controls the reset lines to the GAME.
        satstatus      Displays the Status of the Saturn CARTDEV.
        scsireset      Issues a SCSI device reset command.
        set            Sets one of the variables or state flags in HSITOOL32.
        shell          Executes a DOS command interpreter.
        show           Shows one of the variables or state flags in HSITOOL32.
        status         Displays the status info of the GAME target processor.
        step           Sends the STEP command to a target processor.
        stop           Sends the STOP command to a target processor.
        toaddr         Sends data to CARTDEV memory.
        todpr          Sends buffer to CARTDEV DPR buffer.
        tunr           Sends a SCSI Test Unit Ready command to CARTDEV.
        vdp1           Saturn VDP1 specific commands.
        version        Displays the version information.
        writeb         Writes GAME memory as bytes.
        writel         Writes GAME memory as long words.
        writew         Writes GAME memory as words.
        xit            Exits HSITOOL32.
*

!       Help for HSITOOL32 is in this file HELP.DAT, it must be in the
        directory that HSITL32.EXE is run in or the environment variable
        HSIHPL can be set to the complete pathname and filename of the
        help file to use.

*

!       Command line processing features:

        Version 33.0 added the ability to enter HSI Tool commands on the
        DOS command line. You can type any valid HSI Tool command, including
        executing a script. For example:

            HSITL32 @startup exit

        Will execute the commands in the file startup and then exit the
        HSI Tool utility.

        HSITL32 will also search for a script file HSISTART.SCR, if it 
        finds this file in the current directory it will execute commands
        from that file prior to displaying the command line prompt or 
        executing command line arguments.

        HSITL32 has a command queue, similar to that of DOSKEY. It has
        completion and allows flexible editing of the command line.

        The command queue is accessed by the arrow keys, the UP Arrow key
        scans backward in the queue, the DOWN Arrow key scans forwards in
        the command queue.

        As the queue fills the oldest commands are overwritten.
        
        Once a command line has been selected via the command queue or by
        typing in text you can edit the command line with the following 
        keystrokes:

         ESCAPE      Erases the entire line.
         DELETE      Deletes the character under the cursor.
         BACKSPACE   Deletes the character to the left of the cursor.
         INSERT      Toggles INSERT and OVERSTRIKE mode. When in INSERT mode
                     text is entered in the current cursor position.
                     OVERSTRIKE mode replaces text on the command line.
                     The default is OVERSTRIKE.
         RightArrow  Moves the cursor one character to the right.
         LeftArrow   Moves the cursor one character to the left.
         HOME        Positions the cursor at the beginning of the line.
         END         Positions the cursor at the end of the line.
         ^TAB        Moves the cursor over 8 characters toward the right.
         ^RightArrow Moves the cursor over 1 word toward the right. Whitespace
                     delimeted.
         ^LeftArrow  Moves the cursor over 1 word toward the left. Whitespace
                     delimeted.
         UpArrow     Command queue scan, display forward in the queue.
         DownArrow   Command queue scan, display backward in the queue.

         TAB         Searches the command history for a command that starts
                     with the text you have entered, if one is found the
                     command appears on the command line with the cursor at
                     the end of the line.

         ENTER       The enter key may be pressed from any position on the 
                     command line. When the enter key has been pressed HSITL32
.                    will begin interpreting the command line.
*

!       abort     ABORT
                  The ABORT command sends an ASPI abort SRB to the ASPI
                  driver.
                  
                  This will abort the ASPI command in progress. Note if there
                  are two ASPI commands pending only the last one issued will
                  be aborted.

                  If used improperly this command can make the ASPI/SCSI driver
                  unstable, USE THE ABORT COMMAND carefully!
.
*

!       audioread AUDIOREAD LENGTH FILENAME
                  Reads Digital Audio Data from the CartDev for LENGTH bytes
                  into FILENAME. 

                  The LENGTH is a 32 bit number. If LENGTH is 0 (zero) then 
                  reading will be terminated by a timeout or by pressing the
                  ESCAPE key.

                  FILENAME is any valid DOS filename (in 8.3 format). To
                  create a file on another drive or directory enclose the 
                  complete FILENAME in quotes. If FILENAME exists it will be
                  over-written with the new data.

                  The AUDIOREAD command uses the currently SET timeout value,
                  DPR inbuff number, Audio Read buffer size, and does not 
                  reconfigure the DPR buffers. The SET command can be used to
                  configure the DPR buffers, to change the timeout value and 
                  to configure the size of the buffers used for each SCSI 
                  AUDIO READ command issued. See the SET help by typing: 
                  HELP SET.
.
*

!       batch     BATCH OPTION
                  The BATCH command allows for control of how a script
                  operates.

                  Valid OPTIONS are:
                     rewind count   Executes a script count + 1 times.
                     sample         Terminates the rewind count if ESCAPE pressed.
                  
                  The REWIND option restarts the currently operating script
                  by setting the file pointers back to the beginning. The
                  optional COUNT argument will scan the script COUNT additional
                  times. 

                  After the COUNT parameter is exhausted processing will
                  begin on the next line following the BATCH line in
                  the script file.

                  The SAMPLE option samples the keyboard, if you have pressed
                  the escape key the count used for the REWIND option will
                  be set to 0. 
.
*

!       bp        BP Address
                  --- NOT IMPLEMENTED ---
                  The bp command sets breakpoints.
.
*

!       buffconfig BUFFCONFIG [BUFF_ID]
                  The BUFFCONFIG command displays the configuration of the
                  CARTDEV buffers.
                  The optional BUFF_ID allows you to display the
                  configuration of a specific buffer number.

                  When the BUFF_ID parameter is omitted the configuration
                  of all of the buffers is displayed.
.
*

!       cmpdpr    CMPDPR BUFFER_NUMBER LENGTH
                  The CMPDPR command reads DPR memory and compares it to 
                  the internal buffer for the last TODPR command.

                  The two buffers are compared and if there is a buffer
                  miscompare the CMPDPR command will stop and print a
                  message.

                  If the buffers compared OK, nothing is printed. This allows
                  for repetitive testing of the DPR memory buffers.

                  When a TODPR command is issued the HSITOOL32 uses a staging
                  buffer that is 16K. Because the buffer would be over
                  written in longer transfers you should issue a TODPR of
                  less than 16K, and a CMPDPR of whatever size the TODPR
                  command was or less.

.
*

!       configbuf CONFIGBUF BUFF_ID PARAMETER VALUE  
                  The CONFIGBUF command allow you to set specific values
                  in the CARTDEV buffer configuration table.

                  The BUFF_ID parameter is required and determined which
                  buffer entry will be modified.

                  The PARAMETER option is required and must be one of the
                  following:
                     address        The Address of the buffer in CARTDEV.
                     size           The size of the buffer.
                     xfersize       The SCSI transfer size.
                     semaphore0     Buffer full & buffer full mask flags.
                     semaphore1     Same as above, used if Ping-Pong-ing.
                     pingpong       Enables Ping-Pong buffering.
                     ppingpong      Last accessed half of Ping-Pong buffer.
                     gameint        Interrupt TO the GAME from CARTDEV.
                     cartdevint     Interrupt TO the CARTDEV from the GAME.
                     direction      Direction of buffer.
                     dummy          Not currently used.


                  The specifics of this command require a hardware
                  specification to determine the correct values and
                  polarities.

.
*
!       compare   COMPARE FROM_ADDR_START FROM_ADDR_END TO_ADDR_START
                  Compares memory starting at FROM_ADDR_START to
                  TO_ADDR_START for FROM_ADDR_END - FROM_ADDR_START bytes.
                  Only if there is a miscompare is an error printed.
                  Memory is compared 32 bits at a time, and longword
.                 alignment of starting addresses is enforced.
*

!       controlgm CONTROLGM COMMAND COMMAND_SPECIFIER
                  The CONTROLGM command issues the special control game
                  command to the CARTDEV.
                  The COMMAND and COMMAND_SPECIFIER parameters are both
                  required, and are 8 bit values.
                  The specifics of this command require a hardware
                  specification to determine the correct values an polarities.

.
*

!       delay     DELAY TIMEVAL     
                  Delays for TIMEVAL number of 100ths of a second.
.
*

!       download  DOWNLOAD FILE_SPEC
                  The DOWNLOAD command downloads the file FILE_SPEC to the
                  GAME via CARTDEV. The file must contain contiguous SRecords.
                  If the FILE_SPEC is not in the current directory, the 
                  complete pathname must be quoted.
.
*

!       dump      DUMP [[ADDR] [LEN]]
                  NOT TESTED YET!
                  The DUMP command displays CartDev memory.
                  
                  Memory will be displayed in the currently set radix
                  and base.
                  
.
*

!       enter     ENTER ADDR [byte, byte, byte...]

                  NOT TESTED YET!
                  Edits Game memory.

                  Allows you to modify Game memory a byte at a time.
                  If only the ADDR is specified on the command line, the
                  input will be taken from the keyboard and memory will
                  be edited interactively.

                  If one or more bytes are specified on the command line they
                  will be put into Game memory in the order that they appear
                  on the command line, starting at ADDR.

                  Input to the interactive mode and the command line should
                  be in hexadecimal bytes.
                     
.
*

!       exit      EXIT
                  The EXIT command terminates the current HSITOOL32 session.

                  HSITOOL32 will exit in a orderly fashion, the logfile will
                  be closed and all other pending operations will be
                  terminated.

.                 QUIT, EXIT and XIT are identical.
*

!       flash     FLASH FileName

                  Flash programs the CARTDEV Flash EEPROM. The FileName is a
                  Motorola S-Record image of the ROM code.

                  The ROM is programmed in pages. The total image can be up
                  to 128K in size. 

                  The flash command sends the data to the CARTDEV, verifies
                  the data, instructs CARTDEV to verify the checksum, and then
                  instructs the CARTDEV to program the block. This process 
                  is repeated until the entire EEPROM is programmed.
*

!       fill      FILL From_Start From_End Pattern

                  NOT TESTED YET!
                  The memory between From_Start and From_End is filled with
                  Pattern.

                  Pattern is a 32 bit value.

                  The parameters From_Start, From_End and To_Start
                  should all be DWORD aligned addresses within the
                  Game address space.

                  Alignment will be enforced by the FILL command and
                  appropriate diagnostic messages will be printed if 
.                 the alignment is incorrect.
*

!       fromaddr  FROMADDR ADDRESS LENGTH [@ FILESPEC]
                  Gets data from the CARTDEV memory at ADDRESS. Data is
                  acquired using the READ_BUFFER command to the CARTDEV.

                  The ADDRESS is not processed and must match a valid 
                  address range in the CARTDEV address space.

                  The length is a 32 bit number and represents data typed
                  on the command line. 
                  
                  You can optionally have FROMADDR write a file with the
                  memory starting at ADDRESS. The memory will be read in
                  multiple transactions until the LENGTH parameter is
                  exhausted. If the LENGTH parameter is omitted an error
                  message will be printed.

                  The syntax for this form is:

                     FROMADDR ADDRESS LENGTH @FILESPEC

                  FILESPEC is any valid DOS file name. NO error checking is
                  done on the file name. If the file is not in the current
.                 directory, enclose the complete DOS filespec in quotes.
*

!       fromdpr   FROMDPR BUFFER_NUMBER LENGTH
                  Gets data from the CARTDEV BUFFER_NUMBER. Data is
                  sent using the WRITE_BUFFER command to the CARTDEV.

                  The BUFFER_NUMBER is not processed and must match
                  whatever the CARTDEV firmware expects.

                  The length is a 32 bit number and can exceed the size
                  of physical DPR memory. If the command is sent with
                  a LENGTH parameter greater than the size of the
                  available DPR memory, AND there is no provider of 
                  memory to the CARTDEV, the system can deadlock!
                  
                  You can optionally have FROMDPR write the data read
                  into a file.

                  The syntax for this form is:

                     FROMDPR BUFFER_NUMBER LENGTH @FILESPEC

                  FILESPEC is any valid DOS file name. NO error checking
                  is done on the file name. 
.
*

!       gameint   GAMEINT PARAMETER VALUE
                  The GAMEINT command enables, disables or sets the interrupt
                  to occur only one time.

                  The PARAMETER is one of the GAME interrupts:
                     AIRQ0          AIRQ1          AIRQ2          AIRQ3
                     AIRQ4          AIRQ5          AIRQ6          AIRQ7
                     AIRQ8          AIRQ9          AIRQA          AIRQB
                     AIRQC          AIRQD          AIRQE          AIRQF
                     NMISH2Main     NMISH2Sub      Sound          NMISH2Both
                     NMIAll

                  The value parameter can have one of three values:
                     0  The selected interrupt is disabled.
                     1  The selected interrupt is enabled for 1 interrupt.
.                    2  The selected interrupt is enabled.
*

!       go        GO [PROCESSOR]
                  The GO command sends the GO message to the GAME.
                  The target processor may be set on the command line, if
                  this parameter is missing the default processor is used.
.
*

!       help      HELP [command]
                  Help displays information about the commands in HSITOOL32.
                  
                  If no argument is given on the help command line help
                  will display all of the help text available.
                  
                  Help is not case sensitive and will take the argument
                  or any partial command name in lower or upper case.

                  At any time during the help display you may press the
                  escape key, usually marked ESCAPE, to stop help.

                  ? is identical to typing HELP.
.
*

!       inquiry   INQUIRY [SCSI_ID]
                  Displays the SCSI Inquiry data for SCSI target SCSI_ID.
                  
                  If the parameter SCSI_ID is omitted then the inquiry data
.                 is shown for all targets.

*

!       lastsense LASTSENSE 
                  Prints the sense data from the last SCSI command.
.
*

!       loopback  LOOPBACK [VERBOSE] [COUNT [MAXBUFFSIZE [FORCESIZE]]]
                  Loopback sends a random length buffer, up to MAXBUFFSIZE
                  of random data to the monitor. The data is read back and
                  verified. This continues for COUNT passes or 100 if count
                  is omitted.

                  If the string VERBOSE is found on the command line, the HSI 
                  library loopback call is made with a count of 1 and then a
                  diagnostic is printed to the screen, this continues until
                  COUNT is exhausted.

                  To use the FORCESIZE parameter, both COUNT and MAXBUFFSIZE
                  must be specified. The FORCESIZE fixes the size of the
                  buffers sent to the monitor, instead of the default random
                  length. 

                  The data sent will be random regardless of the
.                 FORCESIZE setting.
*

!       memtest   MEMTEST SIZE COUNT From_Address To_Address

                  Issues a memory test command the Saturn Monitor.

                  Valid options for the SIZE parameter are:
                    BYTE      Test using bytes reads/writes.
                    WORD      Test using word reads/writes.
                    DWORD     Test using dword reads/writes.
                    ALL       Test using byte, word and dword reads/writes.

                  COUNT is the number of iterations to perform.

                  From_Address is the inclusive starting point to begin
                  testing at. To_Address is the end of the test range.

                  Each iteration performs a number of different memory tests
.                 for each size selected.
*

!       move      MOVE From_Start From_End To_Start

                  NOT TESTED YET!
                  The data at the address From_Start is moved to the
                  address To_Start.

                  Data is moved 32 bits at a time.

                  The parameters From_Start, From_End and To_Start
                  should all be DWORD aligned addresses within the
                  Game address space.

                  Alignment will be enforced by the MOVE command and
                  appropriate diagnostic messages will be printed if 
.                 the alignment is incorrect.
*

!       quit      QUIT
                  The QUIT command terminates the current HSITOOL32 session.

                  HSITOOL32 will exit in a orderly fashion, the logfile will
                  be closed and all other pending operations will be
                  terminated.

.                 QUIT, EXIT and XIT are identical.
*

!       readb     READB [PROCESSOR]  ADDR LENGTH [@TO_FILE]  
                  Reads GAME memory with the given size. Starting at ADDR for
                  LENGTH bytes. 
                  
                  If the @TO_FILE argument is present, the output is 
.                 re-directed to that file.
*

!       readl     READL [PROCESSOR]  ADDR LENGTH [@TO_FILE]  
                  Reads GAME memory with the given size. Starting at ADDR for
                  LENGTH bytes. 
                  
                  If the @TO_FILE argument is present, the output is 
.                 re-directed to that file.
*

!       readw     READW [PROCESSOR]  ADDR LENGTH [@TO_FILE]  
                  Reads GAME memory with the given size. Starting at ADDR for
                  LENGTH bytes. 
                  
                  If the @TO_FILE argument is present, the output is 
.                 re-directed to that file.
*

!       register  REGISTER [PROCESSOR]
                  Displays the GAME systems' processor registers.
.
*

!       reqsense  REQSENSE [SCSI_ID LUN]
                  Issues a SCSI Request Sense command to the CARTDEV.

                  If SCSI_ID and LUN are not specified the default SCSI_ID
                  will be used and a LUN of 0.
.
*

!       reset     RESET SCSI_ID

                  Reset the target device on ID SCSI_ID.
.
*

!       search    SEARCH FROM TO PATTERN
                  Searches memory for pattern.

.                 Not tested! 
*

!       set       SET [OPTION] [VALUE]

                  The SET command allows you to configure the operation of
                  the HSITOOL32.

                  Current options:

                    audioreadsize Sets the request size for each SCSI audio 
                              read request.

                    channel   Sets the channel ID to VALUE.

                    debug     Sets internal HSI Tool debugging flags.

                    dump      Sets the size memory will be dumped in.
                              valid VALUE parameters are:
                                 byte     dump as bytes
                                 word     dump as words
                                 dword    dump as dwords
                                 radix    dump in the current radix

                    F1        Sets the function key F1 to a string.
                    F2        Sets the function key F2 to a string.
                    F3        Sets the function key F3 to a string.
                    F4        Sets the function key F4 to a string.
                    F5        Sets the function key F5 to a string.
                    F6        Sets the function key F6 to a string.
                    F7        Sets the function key F7 to a string.
                    F8        Sets the function key F8 to a string.
                    F9        Sets the function key F9 to a string.
                    F10       Sets the function key F10 to a string.
                    F11       Sets the function key F11 to a string.
                    F12       Sets the function key F12 to a string.

                    inbuff    Sets the DPR buffer for HSITOOL32 to receive
                              commands from the GAME.

                    logfile   Sets the filename to use a logfile. The VALUE
                              parameter contains the DOS filename. If the
                              VALUE parameter is absent the current logfile
                              will be closed.

                    outbuff   Sets the DPR buffer for HSITOOL32 to send
                              commands to the GAME.

                    processor Sets the default processor for commands sent
                              to the monitor. Valid processors are:
                                SH2_Main
                                SH2_Sub
                                68000

                    radix     Sets the radix to use as the default for
                              numeric arguments. Valid VALUE parameter values
                              are:
                                 2        base 2  -- binary
                                 8        base 8  -- octal
                                 10       base 10 -- decimal
                                 16       base 16 -- hexidecimal
                              Note: All numeric arguments (including the
                              RADIX!) are subject to the current radix.
                              
                              You may over-ride the base of a numeric
                              argument with explicit base specifiers. eg:
                                 0x1234   hex
                                 0o177    octal
                                 0d999    decimal
                                 0b010101 binary

                    retrycount Sets the number of retries HSI Tool will allow
                              before printing an error messages. This applies
                              for commands that are interpreted by the target
                              monitor, and the AUDIOREAD command.

                    symbols   To set a symbol to a value use the following
                              syntax:

                              SET $name = value

                              A symbol can be used anywhere a numeric value is 
                              required.

                    timeout   Sets the timeout value used by the HSI library when
                              issuing command to the CartDev. If a command takes
                              longer that the timeout value a timeout error will
                              be returned by the HSI library.

                              The timeout value is in 100'ths of a second. The 
.                             library default is 500 or 5 seconds.
*

!       shell     SHELL
                  SHELL allows you to exit to DOS from within HSITOOL32.

                  After typing SHELL, you will see a DOS prompt. To
.                 return to HSITOOL32 type exit.
*

!       show      SHOW OPTION
                  SHOW displays the configuration information that is 
                  SET-able. See the description in SET also.

                  Current options:

                    binaryout   When commands dump data to a file eg: fromaddr, 
                              the output will be binary instead of a hex dump.

                    audioreadsize Diplays the request size for each SCSI audio 
                              read request.

                    channel   Displays the current channel ID.

                    debug     Displays the internal HSI Tool debugging flag.

                    dump      Displays the size memory will be dumped in.

                    F1        Displays the current text assigned to function
                              key F1.
                    F2        Displays the current text assigned to function
                              key  F2.
                    F3        Displays the current text assigned to function
                              key  F3.
                    F4        Displays the current text assigned to function
                              key  F4.
                    F5        Displays the current text assigned to function
                              key  F5.
                    F6        Displays the current text assigned to function
                              key  F6.
                    F7        Displays the current text assigned to function
                              key  F7.
                    F8        Displays the current text assigned to function
                              key  F8.
                    F9        Displays the current text assigned to function
                              key  F9.
                    F10       Displays the current text assigned to function
                              key  F10.
                    F11       Displays the current text assigned to function
                              key  F11.
                    F12       Displays the current text assigned to function
                              key  F12.

                    inbuff    Displays the DPR buffer set to receive commands
                              from the Monitor or other software running on
                              the target.

                    logfile   Displays the state of logging. 

                    outbuff   Displays the DPR buffer set for HSITOOL32 to
                              send commands to the GAME.

                    processor Displays the current setting of the default 
                              processor used in commands to the Monitor or
                              other software running on the target.

                    radix     Displays the current RADIX.

                    retrycount Displays the number of retries HSI Tool 
                              will allow before printing an error messages.
                              
                    symbols   Displays the contents of the symbol table.
                              You can also display the contents of each symbol
                              individually by typing:
                                SHOW $SymbolName
                              where SymbolName is the requested symbol.


                    timeout   Displays the timeout value used by the HSI 
                              library when issuing commands to the CartDev.
*

!       step      STEP [PROCESSOR]
                  The STEP command sends the STEP message to the GAME.
                  The target processor may be set on the command line, if
.                 this parameter is missing the default processor is used.

*

!       toaddr    TOADDR ADDRESS [[LENGTH DATA...] | @ FILESPEC]
                  Sends data to the CARTDEV memory at ADDRESS. Data is
                  sent using the WRITE_BUFFER command to the CARTDEV.

                  The ADDRESS is not processed and must match a valid 
                  address range in the CARTDEV address space.

                  The length is a 32 bit number and represents data typed
                  on the command line. Since the command line is limited to
                  to 80 characters, the number of entries that can be
                  represented is the upper limit of the LENGTH parameter.

                  You can optionally have TOADDR read a file into the
                  memory starting at ADDRESS. The file will be read in
                  multiple transactions until the LENGTH parameter is
                  exhausted. If the LENGTH parameter is omitted the length
                  of the file is used.
                
                  The syntax for this form is:

                     TOADDR ADDRESS [LENGTH] @FILESPEC

                  FILESPEC is any valid DOS file name. NO error checking is
                  done on the file name. If the file is not in the current
.                 directory, enclose the complete DOS filespec in quotes.
*

!       todpr     TODPR BUFFER_NUMBER LENGTH [[+]PATTERN]
                  Sends data to the CARTDEV BUFFER_NUMBER. Data is
                  sent using the WRITE_BUFFER command to the CARTDEV.

                  The BUFFER_NUMBER is not processed and must match
                  whatever the CARTDEV firmware expects.

                  The length is a 32 bit number and can exceed the size
                  of physical DPR memory. If the command is sent with
                  a LENGTH parameter greater than the size of the
                  available DPR memory, AND there is no consumer of the
                  memory from the CARTDEV the system can deadlock!
                  
                  If the PATTERN parameter is present the internal
                  buffer will be filled with PATTERN, the optional '+'
                  preceding the PATTERN parameter will increment the
                  PATTERN data in the internal buffer. The first value
                  will be PATTERN.

                  If PATTERN is a 32 bit number the PATTERN will be
                  replicated 32 bits at a time, and incremented 32 bits
                  at a time. If the PATTERN is a 16 bit number it will
                  be replicated 16 bits at a time. If PATTERN is an 8
                  bit number it will be replicated byte by byte. If
                  the '+' is present the PATTERN will be incremented
                  based on its size.

                  You can optionally have TODPR read a file into the
                  internal buffer. The file will be read repeatedly
                  until the internal buffer is full. The syntax for
                  this form is:

                     TODPR BUFFER_NUMBER LENGTH @FILESPEC

                  FILESPEC is any valid DOS file name. NO error checking
                  is done on the file name. If the file is not in the
                  current directory, enclose the complete DOS filespec in
                  quotes.
.
*

!       tunr      TUNR [SCSI_ID LUN]
                  Sends a SCSI Test Unit Ready command to the CARTDEV.
                  If SCSI_ID and LUN are not specified the default SCSI_ID
                  will be used and a LUN of 0.
.
*

!       vdp1      VDP1 OPTION PARAM...
                  Communicates with the monitor to affect the VDP1 in the
                  Saturn. These options are mainly used for monitor debugging.

                  Valid OPTIONs are:
                    clear       Clear the Saturn display and re-initialize 
                                the VDP1.
                    init        Initialize the VDP1.
                    modr        Prints the contents of the MODR register.
                    pctrl       Controls whether successive print options 
                                clear the Saturn display or not.
                    print       Prints text on the Saturn display.
.
*

!       version   VERSION [ALL]
                  The VERSION command displays the current version 
                  information for HSITOOL32.

                  If the optional text ALL (case insensitive) is found
                  all of the component files and their compilation dates and
.                 times will be printed.
*

!       writeb    WRITEB [PROCESSOR] ADDR [data... | @FROM_FILE]
                  Writes GAME memory with the given size. Starting at ADDR,
                  the length of the write is determined by the data on the
.                 command line, or the size of the optional FROM_FILE.
*

!       writel    WRITEL [PROCESSOR] ADDR [data... | @FROM_FILE]
                  Writes GAME memory with the given size. Starting at ADDR,
                  the length of the write is determined by the data on the
.                 command line, or the size of the optional FROM_FILE.
*

!       writew    WRITEW [PROCESSOR] ADDR [data... | @FROM_FILE]
                  Writes GAME memory with the given size. Starting at ADDR,
                  the length of the write is determined by the data on the
.                 command line, or the size of the optional FROM_FILE.
*

!       xit       XIT
                  The XIT command terminates the current HSITOOL32 session.

                  HSITOOL32 will exit in a orderly fashion, the logfile will
                  be closed and all other pending operations will be
                  terminated.

.                 QUIT, EXIT and XIT are identical.
